            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your 1996 house is in very good shape and is very close to being perfectly in balance (from an air leakage perspective) for the type of mechanical equipment and ventilation systems currently being used. If you proceeded with a DHERP retrofit of your house, I expect there will be some air leakage reductions you would enjoy:



** Air leakage associated with some of the windows &amp; exterior doors (not really significant)



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** Around the dryer vent as it passes through the rear wall



** An intentional hole (the 5" combustion air supply pipe) would be eliminated if a new on-demand, direct vent hot water heater is installed.



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 66 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.9 GJ, and qualify for  500 in rebates. For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

You mentioned that you are currently evaluating an exterior siding (and possibly windows/doors) upgrade to your home &amp; this is an excellent time to add more insulation to the exterior. The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x6 (R20) and I have modeled the addition of another R15 (eg. 3" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of these main floor walls.



An R30 wall would represent a current building code requirement of new housing, however it appears you have always gone above &amp; beyond code in the past (when the house was originally built &amp; when the basement was subsequently insulated/finished inside. Knowing that you have done "a little more" in the past .......... I decided to model an additional R15 of insulation. You have 2' of eave trough overhang, so plenty of room for 3" thicker walls.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). Please keep in mind that, in order to receive the full rebate, you need to add the required insulation to 100% of the perimeter of the main floor walls ......... and this includes the wall that adjoins the garage as heat loss occurs through that wall also.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 337 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

When you add R15 to the main floor walls of the house, this (the insulation &amp; new siding) will extend down over the basement header space. The savings from adding R15 to the exterior of the basement header area has been included with the savings in the Wall Insulation upgrade section of this report.</foundation>
                <windows>

You mentioned that you are currently evaluating an exterior siding upgrade to your home. This would be an ideal time to add more exterior wall insulation and let our new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program help pay for the cost of the new insulation. In conjunction with this, you indicated that you may even consider upgrading some/all of the original windows, and they would be designed with the proper depth jambs to fit into the new thicker walls.



I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of the original windows (main floor and basement) with new ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour. Many new windows today have a U-Value of 0.20 !!!



Savings:

By replacing the current windows (and 2 patio doors) with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 129 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates (12 windows max @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



During my assessment, we talked quite a bit about the original 1996 "Enviro ENV-K6" Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) in your home - including the controller, winter humidity settings and we both eventually wondered .......... is it even working properly ?



As this HRV is now 25 years old and was one of the early generation HRV's on the market, I think it is time you review an upgrade. These older units were lucky to achieve 55% efficiency in the heat transfer between outgoing stale air &amp; incoming fresh air, which means that 45% of the heat inside the house is being sent outside.



Most HRV manufacturers now have really high efficiency HRV's (75% efficient in heat transfer) and many of the new units have high efficiency ECM motors to run the circulation fan (just like your new high efficiency furnace has an ECM blower motor).



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR?????? rated &amp; HVI Certified Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, you could potentially save $ 41 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.7 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 189 litre gas-fired hot water tank (a rated 0.66 Energy Factor), power-vented out the back wall of the basement. Because this hot water tank draws its' combustion air from inside the house, you have a 5" combustion air supply pipe located in your mechanical room.



You have recently upgraded to a super high efficiency gas furnace (95% efficiency), and I thought I would model the opportunity for you to also upgrade this hot water tank to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.92 or higher. This is 40% more efficient than the current tank and part of this higher efficiency is because you are not trying to maintain 189 litres (50 USG) of hot water in a tank in your basement (essentially a big kettle) for when you may need it. Instead - you would have hot water on-demand &amp; only when you need it.



This upgrade would eliminate the need for the 5" combustion air supply pipe. I believe both of your gas fireplaces draw their combustion air directly from outside - if this is true, than YES the 5" pipe in the mechanical room can be removed in conjunction with this hot water heater upgrade.



Savings: By replacing your current gas-fired hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.92 EF, could potentially save $ 64 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem />
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $9,500 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $5,000 for adding R15 to the main floor walls ($8,000 if you add R20)

     - $3,000 for replacing 12 windows with new eligible units

     - $   500 for a 10% air leakage reduction

     - $1,000 for the bonus rebate (a minimum 20% reduction in total gigajoules is possible)



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and none of the rebates above would be available.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - On-Demand Hot Water (minimum 0.92 EF) - $750

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood fan recirculates inside the house, so we turned on the 3 x bathroom fans, the clothes dryer and the central vacuum), indicated a pressure drop of 5.5 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could compete against the chimney to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. There are no chimney vented appliances in your home - the furnace, hot water tank &amp; both gas fireplaces are all power-vented &amp; have dedicated outside combustion air.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The original 1996 "Enviro ENV-K6" Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit

        - A kitchen range hood fan (recirculates inside the house)

        - 3 bathroom fans

        - A clothes dryer

        - The central vacuum vents to the outside of the house



You may want to look at replacing the bathroom exhaust fans with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. You may want to consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



Further ........... you should talk at length with the qualified contractor who installs your new HRV to deterrnine the proper settings for your house (based on house layout and the existing HRV ductwork installed) to see how much you even need to operate the bathroom fans so that you don't over-ventilate your house and have too little humidity inside your house during the heating season.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $0.80 per litre 

Propane - $2.46 per m3 for the piped system; or $0.66 per litre delivered 

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.20 per bag, $268.12 per tonne bulk delivered



